C = [
("h1", "14. The Sayings Clause by Clause"),
("p", "Claim: when the sayings are set in order of Christ’s history, from before the incarnation, through it, to the exaltation, they describe one person at each point. The table in this chapter does not combine the passages into a creed. It puts each saying where its own words put it."),
("sub", "14.1 Before the servant’s form"),
("p", "Four passages speak of Christ in a state before he took flesh. Philippians 2:6 says he was “in the form of God” and was equal with God. Colossians 1:15 to 17 says he is the image of the invisible God and that all things were created by him and for him, and that he is “before all things.” Galatians 4:4 says God “sent forth” his Son, and 2 Corinthians 8:9 says that he was rich before he became poor. Each statement is made in the language of a state that is past, and none says how long that state lasted. The volume reports them as written."),
("sub", "14.2 In the flesh"),
("p", "Four passages speak of him in the flesh. Philippians 2:7 and 2:8 say he took the form of a servant, was made in the likeness of men, and was found in fashion as a man. First Timothy 3:16 says God was manifest in the flesh. Romans 1:3 says he was of the seed of David according to the flesh, and Romans 9:5 says that as concerning the flesh Christ came from the fathers. First Timothy 2:5 calls him the man Christ Jesus. The passages are agreed that the flesh was real. None says that the flesh was the whole."),
("sub", "14.3 After the Cross"),
("p", "Three passages speak of him after. Philippians 2:9 to 11 says God highly exalted him and gave him a name above every name. First Timothy 3:16 says he was received up into glory. Philippians 3:21 speaks of “his glorious body,” and Colossians 3:1 sets him at “the right hand of God.” In none of these does the exalted one cease to be the one who was in the flesh. In Colossians 2:9 the fulness of the Godhead dwells in him bodily, and the verb is in the present."),
("ctbl", "The sayings in the order of Christ’s history.", [
    ("The point", "What the passages say", "Passages"),
    ("Before the flesh", "In the form of God; equal with God; the image of the invisible God; the Creator; before all things; rich; sent forth as Son", "Phil 2:6; Col 1:15–17; 2Cor 8:9; Gal 4:4"),
    ("In the flesh", "Servant’s form; likeness of men; found in fashion as a man; God manifest in the flesh; of the seed of David; the man Christ Jesus", "Phil 2:7, 8; 1Tim 3:16; Rom 1:3; 1Tim 2:5"),
    ("At the Cross", "Obedient unto death, even the death of the cross; the blood of his cross", "Phil 2:8; Col 1:20"),
    ("After the Cross", "Highly exalted; a name above every name; received up into glory; at the right hand of God; the glorious body", "Phil 2:9–11; 1Tim 3:16; Col 3:1; Phil 3:21"),
    ("Now", "In him dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead bodily; head of the body, the church", "Col 2:9; 1:18"),
    ("Named God", "God was manifest in the flesh; the great God and our Saviour; who is over all, God blessed for ever", "1Tim 3:16; Titus 2:13; Rom 9:5"),
  ], [0.17, 0.55, 0.28]),
("sub", "14.4 What the order shows"),
("p", "The order does not claim to put the passages in a time sequence that each of them states. It sets each saying at the point where its verbs put it. The result is that the same person is named at each point. The one who was in the form of God is the one who took the form of a servant, and the one who died on the cross is the one by whom all things were created. Paul does not name a second subject between the stages. The volume holds that this is the plain sense of the sayings read in order, and that it requires no word from later centuries."),
("sub", "14.5 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Four passages speak of him before the flesh, four in it and three after the Cross.",
    "Paul uses the same subject through all of them, without naming a second one between the stages.",
    "The flesh is real in every passage that names it, and none says the flesh was the whole.",
    "The order is set by the verbs of the passages and not by a scheme brought to them.",
  ]),
("p", "The next chapter sets the passages side by side by their problems, which shows what each was written to answer."),
]
